<p>The accompanying analytica conference drew more than 2,000 participants. Sessions covered digital and green laboratories, detection of designer drugs in forensic work, mass spectrometry for establishing the botanical origin of honey, and the analysis of ancient manuscripts.</p>
<h2>Market outlook quoted by the organiser</h2>
<p>The final report says experts expect the global laboratory market to grow by about 5 per cent a year, driven mainly by automation, robotics and investment in life-science, pharmaceutical and environmental analysis. It also describes the mood among exhibitors as optimistic despite a challenging market. These are the organiser's statements and projections, not independent measurements.</p>
<h2>What it means for Indian laboratories</h2>
<p>For buyers in India, the practical signal is the direction of product development: more instruments with software control, remote monitoring and links to laboratory data systems. Labs planning purchases in the next few years may want to ask suppliers about data export, software support and service coverage, not only about the instrument itself.</p>
