        'html' => <<<'HTML'
<p>ISO 15189:2022, the standard for quality and competence in medical laboratories, replaced the 2012 edition. The International Laboratory Accreditation Cooperation (ILAC) announced the new standard on 8 December 2022 and set the timetable for accreditation bodies worldwide [1].</p>
<h2>The transition rule</h2>
<p>ILAC's General Assembly endorsed Resolution GA 26.08, which allows three years from publication for implementation. The ILAC notice gives the deadline as December 2025. When the transition ends, accreditation to ISO 15189:2012 is no longer recognised under the ILAC Arrangement. The same applies to point-of-care testing accredited under ISO 22870:2016 combined with ISO 15189:2012, because the point-of-care requirements of ISO 22870:2016 were incorporated into the revised standard. ILAC said its Arrangement Management Committee would monitor implementation, using a process similar to that used for ISO/IEC 17025:2017.</p>
<p>Individual accreditation bodies may set their own detailed dates within this framework. We have not located NABL's own transition notice, so we do not state NABL's specific cut-off here; medical laboratories should confirm it directly with NABL.</p>
<h2>NABL activity for medical laboratories</h2>
<p>NABL's news page [2] lists several items relevant to medical laboratories since late 2025:</p>
<ul>
<li>NABL 112B, "Guidance document: Medical Laboratories", Issue No. 01 dated 18 December 2024, with Amendment No. 01 dated 4 November 2025, posted on 6 November 2025;</li>
<li>a notice dated 14 October 2025 that NABL-accredited medical labs receive 15 per cent higher rates than non-accredited labs under CGHS;</li>
<li>on 7 May 2026, CGHS eligibility criteria for empanelment of super-specialty hospitals, diagnostic labs and imaging centres;</li>
<li>on 15 October 2026, new documents NABL 237 and 238 for an Integrated Quality Assurance Scheme for NACO network laboratories.</li>
</ul>
<p>The page also carries an announcement that an upfront payment system for NABL accreditation takes effect on 1 October 2026; we have only the headline and have not read the full notice.</p>
<h2>Practical points</h2>
<p>Laboratories should keep their quality manual, risk-management records and equipment calibration and maintenance files aligned with the current standard. Instrument documentation, calibration certificates and service records are often among the first things assessors request, so keep them organised and current.</p>
